            <ArticleTitle>[Mixed aphasia as a dominating symptom of encephalitis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>146-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Encephalitis has typically a severe course, with psychotic disorders, consciousness disorders, epileptic seizures and meningal signs. The author describes a case of a young male, in whom dominant symptoms of encephalities were aphasia and headache. These symptoms were accompanied by considerable inflammatory changes in cerebral fluid. The outcome of illness was favourable.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
